    <div class='about-slide__section' id='fastapi'>
      <div class='about-slide__content'>
        <h3 class='about-slide__heading'>AI answers that survive a refresh</h3>
        <p class='about-slide__text about-slide__text_lead'>
          A tab switch or a network blip ends a streamed LLM answer, and there is nothing to resume from. With AnyCable, your FastAPI worker broadcasts tokens to a stream, and the Go server holds the connection, keeps the history and replays from where the client left off. No WebSocket code in Python, no sticky sessions, no Redis Streams plumbing.
        </p>
        <p class='about-slide__text'>
          GitLab ships AnyCable's JavaScript client in GitLab Duo to stream AI responses.
          <a href='https://docs.anycable.io/guides/python' target='_blank' class='plausible-event-name=Navigation plausible-event-link=fastapi-guide' data-ph-capture-attribute-link-id='fastapi-guide'>Read the Python and FastAPI guide →</a>
        </p>
        <h3 class='about-slide__subtitle'>Works with your stack</h3>
        <p class='about-slide__text'>
          AnyCable runs next to your application. Rails and Laravel get native SDKs; FastAPI, Node.js and any other backend broadcast over HTTP. Hotwire, Turbo Streams and every JavaScript framework work out of the box, and if you already run Redis or NATS, AnyCable can use them.
        </p>
      </div>
